# Project rules — Marianne "Ian" Napaño's portfolio

Vite + React + TypeScript, deployed on Vercel. Read this whole file before
making changes. When a rule here conflicts with what looks "more correct"
in general, follow this file — it reflects deliberate decisions made for
this specific project, not defaults to override.

## Stack & conventions

- Vite (not Next.js) — no `app/` router directory, no `"use client"`
  requirement, no Server Components. Routing is `react-router-dom`
  (`src/App.tsx`), currently `/` and `/projects`.
- Path alias `@/*` maps to the project root. Always import via `@/...`,
  never relative paths like `../../components/...`.
- `tsconfig.json` has `noUnusedLocals` and `noUnusedParameters` enabled.
  An unused import or function parameter is a build error, not a warning —
  remove it, don't just ignore the squiggle.
- Icons: `lucide-react`. Its 1.0 release removed all trademarked brand
  icons (GitHub, etc.) — never `import { Github } from "lucide-react"`,
  it doesn't exist. Use `components/icons/GithubIcon.tsx` (a plain inline
  SVG) for that one; follow the same pattern for any other brand mark.
- Two React Bits components (`components/ui/PixelTransition.tsx`,
  `components/ui/Stack.tsx`) use CSS Modules (`.module.css`), not global
  CSS — that's required for Vite, don't convert them to plain `.css`
  imports or inline Tailwind.

## Design system — do not improvise around this

Everything lives in `src/styles/theme.css` as CSS custom properties.
Never hardcode a hex color, a font-family string, or a shadow value in a
component — reference the token instead (`var(--ink)`, `var(--gray-500)`,
`var(--font-display)`, etc.). If a new color/spacing need comes up, add
the token to `theme.css` first, then use it.

- **No accent color, ever.** The palette is strictly monochrome
  (`--bg`, `--ink`, `--gray-50` through `--gray-950`) plus whatever
  light/dark theming those resolve to. Don't introduce a blue link
  color, a colored badge, a brand hue — emphasis comes from inversion
  (ink-filled chip) or typography, never color.
- **Three font roles, each with a specific job** — don't mix these up:
  - `--font-body` (Geist): paragraph/body copy only.
  - `--font-display` (Geist Pixel, self-hosted in `public/fonts/`):
    headings (`h1`–`h6`, global rule already in `theme.css`), buttons,
    arrow-links, nav labels, the `mrn.` logotype. Has no accented
    characters — don't rely on it for text with diacritics.
  - `--font-mono` (Geist Mono): the tiny uppercase "kicker" register —
    section eyebrows (`01 — home`), `.micro-label`, `.pill` tags. Never
    use the pixel font at that size, it's illegible.
- Reusable classes already exist for common patterns — use them instead
  of rebuilding with raw Tailwind: `.card`, `.pill` / `.pill-inverted`,
  `.btn-primary`, `.link-arrow` (has a built-in hover-animated diagonal
  arrow via `.arrow-glyph`), `.micro-label`, `.section-eyebrow`,
  `.halftone` (dot-texture accent — used sparingly, at most 1-2 spots on
  the whole page, never as general decoration).
- Numbered section eyebrows (`01 — home`, `02 — about`, ...) must stay
  sequential in whatever order the sections actually render in
  `src/App.tsx`. If you reorder, add, or remove a section, renumber
  every eyebrow after that point in the same change.
- Light/dark theming is `data-theme` on `<html>`, toggled via
  `components/layout/ThemeToggle.tsx`, persisted to `localStorage`. The
  theme-switch circle animation uses the View Transitions API, gated
  behind `@supports not (view-transition-name: none)` for the fallback
  CSS crossfade — don't remove that `@supports` guard, both animations
  running together is what caused a lag bug before.

## File organization

- `components/layout/` — sidebar, header, footer, theme toggle, main
  page shell (`MainLayout.tsx`). Layout reserves sidebar space via a
  spacer div in `MainLayout`, NOT via padding on individual sections —
  don't re-add `lg:pl-*` to a section's own className, that duplicates
  the offset (this was a real bug, twice).
- `components/sections/` — one file per homepage section, each a
  default-exported component rendering its own `<section id="...">`.
  Composed in order inside `src/App.tsx`'s `Home` component.
- `components/ui/` — generic, content-agnostic UI primitives (carousel,
  pixel-transition effect, etc.) with no knowledge of this specific
  portfolio's content.
- `components/projects/`, `content/projects.ts`, `pages/ProjectsPage.tsx`
  — the project list has one shared data source (`content/projects.ts`)
  and one shared card component (`ProjectCard.tsx`), reused by both the
  homepage preview (first 4) and the full `/projects` page. Don't fork
  a second copy of the card markup — edit the shared one.
- `api/` — Vercel serverless functions only (currently the Gemini chat
  proxy). Never expose an API key to client code; if a feature needs a
  secret, it goes through a function here.

## Before deleting or restructuring anything

1. Search the whole project for imports of a file before removing it —
   don't assume something's unused from its name or folder alone.
2. Files known to already be genuinely dead, safe to remove if still
   present: any `app/` folder at the project root (pre-Vite leftover),
   `components/ui/DepthCarousel.tsx` + `.module.css` (superseded by
   `Stack.tsx`) — confirm nothing imports them before deleting even
   these.
3. If a cleanup candidate is ambiguous, don't delete it — leave it and
   say so, rather than guessing.
4. Never touch `tsconfig.json`, `vite.config.ts`, or `vercel.json`
   without being asked specifically about them.

## When adding a new homepage section

Follow the existing pattern exactly (look at `components/sections/AboutMe.tsx`
as the reference example):
1. New file in `components/sections/`, default export, `<section id="...">`.
2. `<p className="section-eyebrow">NN — name</p>` as the first element —
   renumber later sections if inserting in the middle.
3. Heading uses a semantic `<h2>` (picks up the display font
   automatically via the global rule).
4. Body copy in plain `<p>` tags (picks up `--font-body` automatically —
   don't set `fontFamily` explicitly unless deliberately deviating).
5. Import and place it in `src/App.tsx`'s `Home` component in the right
   order; add a matching entry to `NAV_ITEMS` in
   `components/layout/Sidebar.tsx` with a `lucide-react` icon (or a
   custom icon component if it's a brand mark).